{{!
    @template local_unifiedgrader/help_section_forum

    Help page partial: marking a forum. The grader's help button links to
    #integration-forum, so that id must stay.

    Context variables:
    * img - object - Screenshot URLs keyed by file name in pix/help.

    Example context (json):
    {
        "img": {
            "forum_ratings": "https://moodle.example.com/theme/image.php/boost/local_unifiedgrader/1/help/forum_ratings",
            "forum_whole": "https://moodle.example.com/theme/image.php/boost/local_unifiedgrader/1/help/forum_whole"
        }
    }
}}
<section id="integration-forum" class="local-unifiedgrader-help-activity">
<h4 class="local-unifiedgrader-help-activity-title"><i class="fa fa-comments-o" aria-hidden="true"></i> Forums</h4>

<p>A Moodle forum can be graded in two ways. The grader reads the forum's settings and shows the
matching controls. If both are set, whole-forum grading is used.</p>

<div class="local-unifiedgrader-help-table">
<table class="table-reboot">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Forum setting</th><th scope="col">What you do in the grader</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Whole forum grading</th><td>Read the student's posts and type one grade (or use a rubric or marking guide).</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Ratings</th><td>Rate each post. Moodle works out the grade from the ratings.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Neither</th><td>Write feedback and switch <strong>Mark as graded</strong> on. There is no grade.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h5>Read the posts three ways</h5>
<p>Use the <strong>Post view</strong> switch above the preview. Your choice is remembered for each forum.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>All posts</strong>: this student's posts, grouped by discussion, oldest first. This is
        also what the student sees on their feedback page.</li>
    <li><strong>In context</strong>: one post at a time, with the discussion prompt, the post it
        answered, the other replies to that post, and the replies it received. Step through the
        student's posts with the arrows.</li>
    <li><strong>Thread</strong>: the whole discussion in reading order, with this student's posts highlighted.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each post shows its word count. The context views are for markers only. Rated forums open on
<em>In context</em>; other forums open on <em>All posts</em>.</p>

<figure class="local-unifiedgrader-help-shot">
    <a href="{{img.forum_whole}}" data-action="help-zoom">
        <img src="{{img.forum_whole}}" width="1600" height="773" loading="lazy"
             alt="Whole-forum grading. On the left the Post view switch (All posts, In context, Thread) and the student's two posts with word counts. On the right a grade box out of 100, the feedback editor and teacher notes.">
    </a>
    <figcaption>Whole-forum grading: the student's posts on the left, one grade and feedback on the right.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h5>Mark a rated forum</h5>
<p>On a rated forum the grade box and rubric are hidden. In their place is a
<strong>Post ratings</strong> list. Penalties are offered only when the ratings can take them
(see below). Late penalties are off on a rated forum until you tick <strong>Apply late
penalties</strong> in the forum's settings, so they apply only where you chose them from the
start.</p>
<ul>
    <li>There is one row per post, each with a rating menu. Above the list is the result the gradebook will receive.</li>
    <li>The menu holds <em>your</em> rating. The figure beside it is the combined rating from everyone who rated that post.</li>
    <li>Choosing <em>Rate…</em> again removes your rating. It does not score the post as zero.</li>
    <li>A student counts as graded once every one of their posts has a rating.</li>
    <li>You cannot rate your own posts.</li>
    <li>Ratings have no comment of their own. Use <strong>Overall Feedback</strong> for a comment to the student.</li>
</ul>
<p>Clicking a row in the list shows that post in the preview, and clicking a post selects its row.</p>

<figure class="local-unifiedgrader-help-shot">
    <a href="{{img.forum_ratings}}" data-action="help-zoom">
        <img src="{{img.forum_ratings}}" width="1600" height="818" loading="lazy"
             alt="A rated forum in the In context view. The student's post is outlined between the discussion prompt, another reply and a follow-up. On the right, a Post ratings list shows one post rated 8 and one not yet rated, with the average sent to the gradebook.">
    </a>
    <figcaption>A rated forum: the post in context on the left, the <strong>Post ratings</strong> list on the right.</figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="local-unifiedgrader-help-note">
    <p><strong>Which rated forums take penalties.</strong> A penalty is a percentage of the maximum
    grade taken off the grade. That needs a grade that is arithmetic, so a rated forum takes
    penalties only when its ratings are <em>points</em> (not a scale such as stars or words) and the
    <em>Aggregate type</em> is <em>Average of ratings</em> or <em>Sum of ratings</em>. With
    <em>Count of ratings</em> the grade only tallies participation, and with <em>Maximum rating</em>
    or <em>Minimum rating</em> a single post decides the whole grade, so a late penalty would mean
    nothing. On any other rated forum the <strong>Penalties</strong> button and the final grade line
    are hidden and no penalty is taken.</p>
    <p>On a forum that takes them, the penalty comes off the combined rating, never below zero. The
    ratings themselves are untouched: the gradebook keeps the combined rating and records the
    deduction beside it, and puts it back whenever a rating changes. If you change the
    <em>Aggregate type</em> or the rating scale so that the forum no longer qualifies, the
    deductions are removed again.</p>
</div>

<h5>Due dates, extensions and late posts</h5>
<p>With a due date, a student is late when their first post comes after the due date. This applies
to whole-forum grading, and to a rated forum that takes penalties.
<a href="#feature-penalties">Late penalties</a> apply when the forum has
<em>Apply late penalties</em> ticked, and you can give one student more time from
<a href="#feature-overrides">Dates &amp; extensions</a>. Your grade is stored as you typed it; the
penalty is recorded beside it in the gradebook. On a rated forum the same goes for the combined
rating.</p>

<p>If a plagiarism plugin is in use, each post shows its result, and attachments are listed in the
<strong>Plagiarism</strong> card on the right (see <a href="#feature-referral">Academic integrity</a>).</p>
</section>
